Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 11:30:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Kompakt: a slider change in auto scales the frontlight curve

A slider change while auto brightness is on adds a user point, and stock
inserts it into the curve and smooths everything above it so the curve
never falls. 0146's frontlight curve falls to off in bright light, so
after one slider change the light stayed at the user's level up to
sunlight (812 lux measured at a window, light held at 41%).

SimpleMappingStrategy now scales the whole curve by the ratio of the
user's brightness to the curve at the user's lux, so it passes through
the user's point and keeps its shape, including off. Where the curve is
below its lowest step at that lux the scale would be huge, and the stock
adjustment is used.
---
diff --git a/services/core/java/com/android/server/display/BrightnessMappingStrategy.java b/services/core/java/com/android/server/display/BrightnessMappingStrategy.java
--- a/services/core/java/com/android/server/display/BrightnessMappingStrategy.java
+++ b/services/core/java/com/android/server/display/BrightnessMappingStrategy.java
@@ -821,11 +821,36 @@
         }
 
         private void computeSpline() {
-            Pair<float[], float[]> curve = getAdjustedCurve(mLux, mBrightness, mUserLux,
-                    mUserBrightness, mAutoBrightnessAdjustment, mMaxGamma);
+            Pair<float[], float[]> curve = kompaktScaleToUserPoint();
+            if (curve == null) {
+                curve = getAdjustedCurve(mLux, mBrightness, mUserLux,
+                        mUserBrightness, mAutoBrightnessAdjustment, mMaxGamma);
+            }
             mSpline = Spline.createSpline(curve.first, curve.second);
         }
 
+        // Kompakt: a user point scales the whole curve, so a frontlight curve still falls to off.
+        private Pair<float[], float[]> kompaktScaleToUserPoint() {
+            if (mUserLux == INVALID_LUX || !(mUserBrightness >= 0)) {
+                return null;
+            }
+            float lowest = Float.MAX_VALUE;
+            for (float b : mBrightness) {
+                if (b > 0) lowest = Math.min(lowest, b);
+            }
+            final float base = getUnadjustedBrightness(mUserLux);
+            // Where the curve is off or nearly so, a scale would be huge; stock handles it.
+            if (!(base >= lowest)) {
+                return null;
+            }
+            final float scale = mUserBrightness / base;
+            final float[] brightness = new float[mBrightness.length];
+            for (int i = 0; i < brightness.length; i++) {
+                brightness[i] = MathUtils.constrain(mBrightness[i] * scale, 0, 1);
+            }
+            return Pair.create(mLux, brightness);
+        }
+
         private float getUnadjustedBrightness(float lux) {
             Spline spline = Spline.createSpline(mLux, mBrightness);
             return spline.interpolate(lux);
